Z||000000||Neal||11-14-2001||01:30 AM||||first the short background, 3.4 oil pump/pickup will NOT work with a 2.8 pan.  The 2.8 pan is to shallow, by about 1/2+ inch.<P>Solutions:<BR>A) use 2.8 pump and pickup, MUCH less oil flow, not the best choice.<P>B) 3.4 camaro pan $240 (local dealer), not unless i can help it.<P>C) Modify the 2.8 pan.  Cut the bottom area by the pickup out weld a deeper section in.  And redo all the baffels.<P>Im really considering C, I dont have much to loose but time and a few bucks in the sheet metal.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Nashco||11-14-2001||02:58 AM||||Well, if you feel confident with C), why not D)just make an entire new pan from sheet metal?  Probably take just as long in time, plus drilling the holes.<P>Then there's always option E)Get a pan from the junkyard and save time, money, and have a little more piece of mind <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||11-14-2001||09:59 AM||||Or how about f)...   Use the high volume oil pump and a 2.8 pickup???   lots of oil, everything is factory (no modifying) and the engine loves it.  I know I did it to my 2.8.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||11-14-2001||08:12 PM||||D) make whole new pan, it would actually be lots harder than C). as i was just thinking cut an area out and weld a tin pan in.  and the baffels shouldnt be to hard to redo.<P>E) If i could get a used pan for cheap my problem would be solved but no yards around my area have what i need (or none ive checked with.<P>F) nope, the high volume 2.8 pump might match the stock 3.4 capacity, as the pickup tube fot the 3.4 is MUCH bigger.  And im using a high volume 3.4 pump so the oil flow will be the most, even if it costs more...||||reg||1
